WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.088
ROY FULLER: Ready to roll.

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:04.130
NEVIN HEINTZE: All right.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.060
Thanks very much, Roy.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.980
And thanks, everyone,
for your time.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.000
And welcome to this
session on Spanner POCs.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:11.930
I'm Nevin Heintze.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.510
I'm a Senior Engineer at
Google on the Spanner team.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.350
So today, we're
going to discuss how

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.260
to plan and run a Spanner POC.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.420
And the talk is
roughly in three parts.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.640
First, we'll talk
about POCs in general,

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.800
why you want to do a
POC, what would you

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.110
learn from it, what are
some of the typical goals,

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.030
and how you plan one.

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.550
And next, to make this
a bit more concrete,

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.770
I'm going to walk
through an example

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.800
of some typical steps in a POC.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.530
And I'm going to use
this using sample gaming

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:50.720
application that's loosely
based on NFL fantasy football.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.810
And then part 3, I'll conclude
with some observations

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.330
on best practices
and tooling and then

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:59.970
open it up for questions.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.120
All right, so let's
take some context first.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.680
So if we zoom out
a little, then it's

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.860
useful to recognize
the role of a POC

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.130
as part of the big picture
of the Spanner migration.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:18.260
So usually, the POC is fairly
early on in a migration.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.280
I've got on this slide a typical
set of steps of a migration.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.890
And not all migrations
have exactly these steps

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:25.940
in exactly this order.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.650
But in general, this
flow is pretty common.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.990
These steps are fairly
independent of the source

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.372
and target databases.

00:01:34.372 --> 00:01:36.080
And I'm sure for some
of you, these steps

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.338
will be pretty familiar.

00:01:38.338 --> 00:01:40.130
What I'm going to do
is go over these steps

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.460
and highlight them from the
perspective of migrations

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:44.090
to Spanner.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:49.410
So most migrations start with
some kind of assessment phase.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.650
So the goal here is to
determine if the target

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:55.110
database, in this case, Spanner,
is likely to be a good fit.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.170
Will it provide the
necessary functionality?

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.140
Will it meet cost
performance requirements?

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.210
How much work will
the migration be?

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.630
What are the biggest risks?

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.580
What are the biggest benefits?

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.259
So the next step is
often a proof of concept.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:13.380
And in many ways, this is like
an extension of the assessment.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.380
So it's typical for
the proof of concept

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.380
to focus on key items that
came up in the assessment,

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.530
like the biggest
risks or confirming

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.940
the main benefits
can be delivered.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.410
So a proof of concept is
often like a feasibility study

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:28.640
for the full migration.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.850
We want to use it as a
vehicle to understand,

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.090
to explore key technical
issues and tradeoffs,

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.160
and basically to
set up a blueprint

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.980
for the rest of the migration.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.970
All right, so what are
the remaining steps

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.670
of a migration after POC?

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.490
And I've split this into a
couple of steps on this slide.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.190
So in step 3 on this
slide, it's kind

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:53.380
of the core part of the
migration preparation,

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.060
so design, implementation
of the migration.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.290
So, you know, this can
have many components.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.130
And there's some
common themes here--

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.520
designing Spanner
schema, figuring out

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.610
how to move data from
your current database

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.560
into Spanner,
building components

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.110
to support that
move, and the third

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.200
is application changes to
switch over to using Spanner.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.920
So it's typical to
prototype approaches

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.700
to much of this work in the POC.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.060
Now the next step, step 4,
is testing and dry runs.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.600
And successful migrations
involve significant testing,

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.870
either using test traffic or
parts of your production load.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:35.050
And it's common for steps
3 and 4 to be intermixed.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.050
So you might have an initial
design and implementation,

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.380
try it out, find issues,
go back, do some redesign,

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.770
change the implementation.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.070
So this is really an
iterative process.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:49.030
And finally, many migrations
have kind of a target day

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.580
for moving data or
completing a data move

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.410
and switching over
to using Spanner.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:56.740
So this is a really critical
day of a convergence

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:58.540
of all of the
planning and execution

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.520
phases of a migration.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:01.990
And a key part of
this is knowing

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.690
how to respond if things
don't go according to plan.

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:07.600
What signals and dashboards
should we be looking at?

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.370
What's the criteria
for reverting?

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:10.600
How do we revert?

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.420
So if you'd like to hear
more detailed discussion

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.280
of migration, then you
might be interested

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.660
in the talk in a previous Cloud
Spanner meetup back on July 22

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.040
on exactly this topic.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.655
But today, we're going
to focus on POCs.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.460
And I'm going to start
with the kind of core

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.220
question, why do we do a POC?

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.250
So as I mentioned
earlier, in many ways,

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.610
a POC is an extension of
the assessment phase, a kind

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.530
of in-depth, hands-on analysis.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:49.460
So what drives the POC is
usually the open issues, items

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.020
of concern from the assessment.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.250
So what you want to
get out of the POC

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.950
is confidence that the
migration will work

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:00.210
and that it will
be cost effective.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:04.880
So typically, you're looking
to derisk core issues.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.460
Those might be
related to handling

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.740
of load, performance latency.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.950
And we'll look into this in
more detail in the next slide.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.400
Or you might want to validate
the main benefits you think

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.100
you'll get from Spanner,
so to confirm that Spanner

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.910
will scale to your workload.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.660
So POCs, as I mentioned
earlier, are often

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.000
a mini dry run of a migration.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.790
They help identify issues
you might have missed.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:33.350
And they provide data to better
plan out the full migration.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:39.470
Another key role
of POCs is sizing.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.800
So how large will your
Spanner instance need to be?

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.290
How will you manage sizing for
peak versus drops in traffic

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:48.860
and so on?

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:52.370
So typically, I'm going
to have some time where

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:55.490
I have way more traffic and, at
other times, significantly less

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:56.030
traffic.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.970
How am I going to manage those
peaks and troughs in traffic?

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:03.530
So data from a POC is
often critical to coming up

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:06.560
with accurate costing estimates.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.340
And finally, POCs serve to
help you learn about Spanner

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:11.690
and use it effectively.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.500
So let's talk about
some concrete goals.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:26.380
Probably the most basic
and frequent POC goal

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.780
is a very fundamental one--

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.030
does Spanner scale
to my use case?

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.470
Now, as I'm sure
many of you know,

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:36.790
a Spanner database is not
just a single machine or even

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:37.720
a couple of machines.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.020
It's hundreds, thousands, and
in some cases, tens of thousands

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:44.560
of machines working together
to provide a massive, strongly

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:45.790
consistent database.

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:49.930
So the ability to harness
a mass of machines

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.240
and make them behave
like one system

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.480
and at the same time support
serialized asset transactions

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:56.500
in SQL--

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.020
it's really one of the
defining properties of Spanner.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:04.090
Now, many customers want
to see that in action

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.430
and understand how
it'll work for them.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:10.670
And POCs are a really
good way to do that.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.250
A related question
is load surges.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.800
Can Spanner scale up
to surges and then

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:17.810
scale back down again
when there's less load?

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.640
How quickly does this happen?

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.290
And operationally,
what does it look like?

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.020
Next is latency.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.990
Many POCs focus on latency
for key operations.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:31.140
They could be reads or writes,
some kind of analytic queries,

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:32.760
or a combination
of all of these.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.160
And you may have particular
latency percentiles

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.850
that you want to achieve,
maybe a 90th or 95th percentile

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.240
or 99th percentile.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.510
So again, this can be
another key goal of a POC.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.220
Another common question is,
how many nodes do I need?

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.580
So this really
represents a tradeoff.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.430
You can run Spanner a bit
hotter and use less nodes

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.850
but see slightly higher
latency and have less headroom.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.610
Or you can run
Spanner a bit cooler

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:03.300
and use more nodes but see lower
latency and have more headroom.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.260
Now, we strongly recommend
keeping Spanner utilization

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.950
for high-priority
operations under 65%.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:16.170
But it's not uncommon to see
utilization around 35% to 50%

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:19.335
because of the latency and
headroom benefits it delivers.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:26.020
All right, so POCs
can also be used

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.160
to understand the scope
of application changes

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.240
that might be needed
in a migration.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:34.090
So what code changes
will I need to make

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:38.080
to move from, say, MySQL
or Postgres to Spanner?

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.179
Usually, the focus is on key
components of an application.

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:46.030
And the goal of
the POC is to get

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:49.600
that particular specific
component running on Spanner.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.280
And the reason for
doing that is we

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.040
don't want to get all of
the pieces of an application

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:55.930
running during the POC
because that's just

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:58.270
going to be too much work.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:02.500
Finally, a POC can be used to
investigate schema tradeoffs.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.080
For example, what
indexes should I use?

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.660
How will they impact
my read latency?

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.790
And moreover, how
will the indexes

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:10.990
impact my write latency?

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:19.320
OK, a couple of high-level
comments on planning a POC--

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.810
so it's really
important to establish

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.420
a clear scope for a POC.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.700
Don't try to address all
issues or solve all problems.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.280
So it's critical
to limit the scope

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:32.550
to just several important
issues, maybe three or four

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:33.660
issues.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.910
And of course, having
well-defined and realistic

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.100
success criteria is important.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.000
Now, usually, you want
to take a core component

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.940
of your application stack
as the focus of the POC.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.080
It could be your main workload,
maybe your highest QPS workload

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.450
or the workload you're most
concerned about for one

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:52.800
reason or another.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.830
And that drives the
implementation part of the POC.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.050
So the goal here is to
get that component up

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:59.850
and running on Spanner.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:03.510
Or maybe you'll build a mock of
that part of the application,

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:06.720
modeling key aspects of
that application component

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.130
and get that running on Spanner.

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:13.220
The main part of a
POC will typically

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:16.010
be running this
component against Spanner

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.030
using sample traffic.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.900
You might have production
traffic that you can replay.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.780
Or maybe you already
have a load generator.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:25.730
Or perhaps setting
up a load generator

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:29.210
will be a core part of your POC.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:32.660
For this, it's common to use
tools like JMeter or Locust

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:34.100
as load generators.

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:40.480
All right, we're now
going to switch gears,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.590
and I'm going to make
all of this more concrete

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:48.370
by looking at a sample POC.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.440
And I'm going to
use a fantasy sports

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:51.850
game as a running example.

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:53.470
And in particular,
I'm going to base

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:56.850
this on NFL's fantasy football.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.290
I suspect some of
you are already

00:10:58.290 --> 00:10:59.490
quite familiar with this.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.440
But many of you won't
be, so I'm going

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:05.100
to start out by
briefly introducing

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.950
some of the game structure
to get us up and going.

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:10.650
I'm then going to
discuss our POC goals

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.500
and describe the
schema we'll use.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.720
And then I'm going
to do a brief recap

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.750
on some of the
internals of Spanner

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.390
that are going to be
relevant to the POC

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:22.860
that we're going to run.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.260
And then I'm going to
discuss three experiments.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.315
We're going to run and
discuss three experiments.

00:11:28.315 --> 00:11:29.190
So let's get started.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:38.280
So he's a one-pager on
NFL fantasy football.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:41.310
It's somewhat oversimplified
to fit on one slide,

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:45.250
but it has the core
parts of the game.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:50.080
So basically, at the start
of the season, you as a user

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.310
playing this game, you get
to pick a team of 16 players.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:57.950
And this is usually via
something we call the draft.

00:11:57.950 --> 00:11:59.710
And that's a process
in and of itself,

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.200
and we're not going to
focus on that for the POC.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:06.510
We'll just assume that
everyone has their 16 players.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:10.650
Now, the core part of the
game is that each week,

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:14.290
you get to pick nine starters.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:17.560
You pick a quarterback,
you pick two running backs,

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:20.750
you pick two receivers,
and so on down this list.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:24.160
So out of your 16 players,
you pick nine starters.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:27.970
So this leaves you with seven
players that are on the bench.

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:31.210
And those seven basically
are ignored for that week.

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:41.860
So there were some rules
about the deadlines for when

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:43.750
you have to pick your starters.

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:45.400
In essence, you've
got to pick them

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:46.650
before they play that week.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.760
I'm going to skip over
the details of this

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.230
since they aren't really
important for our POC.

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:54.400
But just note that
there is a deadline

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.740
and that that means
we'll have frantic users

00:12:56.740 --> 00:12:59.380
making last-minute
changes to their starters

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.630
before the deadline.

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:04.540
All right, so the next
key part of the game

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.340
is how does scoring work.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.770
So you pick your starters,
and your starters

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:09.880
are really key to this.

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:13.330
Basically, every
starter scores points

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:18.160
based on how they do in
that week's NFL games.

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:21.670
All of this is based on
the official NFL stats.

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:23.830
And then your team
score is just the sum

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.380
of the scores for
all of your starters.

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:30.580
And you win that week if you
score more than other teams

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:31.520
in your league.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:33.550
So just a quick
word on leagues--

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:36.250
typically, you
have 8 to 10 teams

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:39.040
in a league, 8 to 10 users
competing against each other

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:40.970
with their teams in the league.

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:47.370
But there are tens to hundreds
of thousands of leagues.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:51.480
Now, every week,
you can also change

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.790
some of your 16
players via trading

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:56.160
with other teams in your league.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.433
And I'm going to skip
over that for the moment.

00:13:58.433 --> 00:14:00.100
We're not really going
to focus on that.

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:03.630
So there are a number of
parts of the puzzle here,

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:06.720
and we're going to focus
on just one for our POC,

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:09.010
and that's handling
team updates.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.510
So as I mentioned,
each week, users

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:14.940
get to pick nine starters.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:18.360
That translates to traffic
each week from users

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:22.530
to change their starters
and, for example,

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:25.640
switch in a player who is
currently on the bench.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:28.070
And we expect to see
bursts of this traffic

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:29.390
before games start.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:32.060
For example, users might
get fresh information

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.180
about player injuries, weather
conditions, actual team

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.440
lineups, et cetera.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:39.260
And they'll probably want to
make last-minute updates based

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.108
on this new information.

00:14:41.108 --> 00:14:42.650
We're also going to
assume that we're

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:45.260
handling this for
all users across tens

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:47.090
to maybe hundreds of
thousands of leagues.

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:48.633
So as you can
imagine, this is going

00:14:48.633 --> 00:14:50.300
to generate quite a
lot of team traffic.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.610
So let's switch to POC goals.

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:00.085
What are we going to set
as our goals for this POC?

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.760
Probably the most
important goal for our POC

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.320
is, can we handle the
expected team traffic?

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.990
So let's suppose that today,
we see bursts of maybe 5,000

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:16.830
to 10,000 QPS, so
update team requests.

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:19.080
And we may be struggling
to handle those bursts.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:22.800
Maybe we're already running
on a sharded MySQL database.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:26.520
So a core part of our motivation
for running on Spanner

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.810
is to scale easily
for our future growth.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:32.970
Call it scalability insurance.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:37.290
So we could pick maybe 2x
of our existing QPS burst,

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:41.280
or maybe 5x, maybe
50x for our POC.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:45.600
From a Spanner perspective, it
doesn't really matter too much.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:48.960
Internally at Google, we
have Spanner databases that

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:51.390
run many, many millions of QPS.

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:53.880
And it's a matter of
provisioning the right number

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.950
of Spanner resources.

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:59.770
For this POC, we're going
to pick a 5x growth.

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:02.280
So that means our
goal is to handle

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:06.150
traffic of around about 50,000
QPS of team update request

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:08.060
traffic.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:11.690
I've added two other
goals to our POC.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.930
First, we want to verify
that our read and write

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:17.450
latencies are in
acceptable ranges,

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:20.000
let's say under 5
milliseconds per read,

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.360
15 milliseconds for
rates, both at medium

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:28.060
and around about double
that for 99th percentile.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:30.010
And the second
additional goal is

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:32.860
we want to understand how
quickly Spanner can scale up

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:36.640
if we need to handle
a burst of traffic.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:42.210
Now, note that in Spanner,
we can choose a single region

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:44.310
or a multiregion configuration.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.260
And the main advantage
of multiregion

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:47.730
is higher availability.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:51.480
So you go from four
nines for a single region

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:54.240
to five nines for multiregion.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:57.360
And the tradeoff is cost, so
a multiregion configuration

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:59.160
is going to be more
expensive and basically

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:01.560
have more replicas of
your data, and writing

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:03.450
will be a little bit slower.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.520
So for our POC,
we're going to be

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.634
using a single-region
Spanner instance.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:18.510
OK, so now let's
switch to our POC plan.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.339
As we've discussed,
our main focus

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:25.069
is on team update traffic, and
in particular, handling bursts

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:27.290
of this traffic that
we typically see right

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:30.230
before games start as users
make last-minute changes

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:31.980
to their starters.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:36.230
We're going to limit our
POC to the following scope.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:39.050
We're going to use a population
of a million users, randomly

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:40.040
generated.

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:43.280
We're going to just focus
on team update traffic.

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:45.290
And we're going
to limit ourselves

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:47.460
to looking at week
1 of the season.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:49.850
So we'll come back to
these decisions later.

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:53.090
The point here is that
when you're running a POC,

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:55.370
you need to fix many parameters.

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:57.620
And at some level,
they're educated guesses.

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:00.080
And they will often
evolve during the POC.

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:04.540
But you need to start somewhere
or other to get bootstrapped.

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:09.010
And the structure of our run,
our experiments in the POCs--

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:10.520
basically two steps.

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:12.970
Step 1, we're going
to generate and load,

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:15.850
a million users and
their initial teams.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:19.220
And then we're going to hit
our database with 50,000

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:22.790
QPS of team update traffic.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:27.670
All right, next, let's
talk about the tables

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:30.280
we need to set up
to run this POC.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:32.330
So we're going to
have two tables.

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.780
The first is a Users table.

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:39.370
And the primary
key here is UserId.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.790
This table also has a user's
name and email address

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.010
and other information
we might need.

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:47.500
May potentially including
information about how they

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:49.150
log in and authenticate to us.

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:51.040
We're going to gloss over those.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:52.825
While getting the
details of this right

00:18:52.825 --> 00:18:56.980
is critical for an actual
migration, for the POCs,

00:18:56.980 --> 00:18:59.000
these issues often
aren't our focus.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:03.380
And in this POC, we're going
to largely ignore them.

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:05.840
In effect, we're going
to treat this data

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.120
as basically a blob
of a few hundred bytes

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.500
that we have to carry around.

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:11.630
But the content of
these bytes isn't

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:14.270
going to impact the running
or the performance of the POC.

00:19:18.650 --> 00:19:21.710
The next table is
the Teams table.

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:24.590
This is a bit more
interesting for the POC.

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.610
Here is where we store
details of a user's team,

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:30.110
so their quarterbacks,
their running

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:32.720
backs, their wide receivers,
and also the seven

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:35.410
other players on the bench.

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:37.403
I'm just putting
it on this slide

00:19:37.403 --> 00:19:39.070
I've dropped some of
those other fields,

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:41.560
but we've already
talked about what

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:43.910
components we need to
represent a user's team.

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:47.500
So just slot them
into the schema here.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:56.680
All right, so basically, we
need all of the information

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:03.190
that we need to capture a
user's roster in this table.

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:07.510
Now let's focus on the primary
key used by this table.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:09.730
First, we need to have UserId.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:10.930
That's pretty clear.

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:15.490
Now, I mentioned that
users play in leagues.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:19.090
It's possible, and it's actually
pretty common, for a user

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:20.950
to play in multiple leagues.

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:24.740
So we're going to have LeagueId
as a second part of the key

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:27.430
so that we can keep
track of a user's teams

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:29.890
in multiple leagues.

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:32.260
The third component is Week.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:35.650
Now, the NFL season
runs for 17 weeks

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.620
and, well, actually it's
going to be 18 weeks starting

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:40.690
this year, plus playoffs.

00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:44.590
Now, we've chosen here
to capture a user's teams

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:46.540
for each week of the season.

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:49.300
Strictly speaking, we
don't need to do this,

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:53.710
but it's actually a useful
chunk of information

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:57.730
to have because it allows us
to track how a user changes

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:01.030
their team over the course of
a season from week to week.

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:04.000
And it potentially allows us to
do some additional validation

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:07.720
checks and potentially
some additional analytics

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:10.150
and analysis queries.

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:13.700
All right, two final
points on that schema--

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:19.760
first, I've set this up
as an interleave table,

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:23.420
so the Teams table is
interleaved in the Users table.

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:26.900
And this means that a user's
teams will be located together

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:28.640
with the user's data.

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:31.730
So it allows us to do efficient
joins between those two tables

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:32.780
if needed.

00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:36.080
It also allows for easy
cleanup when we delete a user.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:39.020
All of their teams will
be automatically deleted

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:41.820
because of this clause.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:46.260
Now, in the particular scenario
we're using for our POC today,

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:48.570
we're not really going
to be running queries

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.430
where the interleaved
tables are going to be

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:51.940
important for performance.

00:21:51.940 --> 00:21:54.390
However, just note that there
are many situations where

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:57.640
interleaved tables can lead to
significant performance gains,

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:00.510
both for reads and for writes.

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:05.830
All, right the second point I
want to emphasize is on UserId.

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:09.810
So this is a really critical
part of our primary key.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:13.770
So UserId is here, the first
component of primary key.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:16.590
And it's a critical part of
the primary key for both Users

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:18.010
and for Teams tables.

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:19.530
Now, in the previous
slide, we said

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:22.350
we were going to use
random user ID generation.

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:25.110
And this is a really
good way of achieving

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:29.100
a uniform distribution of
IDs across the key spaces

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.580
for both tables.

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:32.760
This is a recommended
Spanner best practice

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:34.860
and in particular
avoids hot spots

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:37.830
and makes scalability easy.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:41.310
To explain why, I
want to briefly recap

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:44.670
an important Spanner,
concept that of splits.

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:47.620
I'm sure some of you are
already very familiar with this.

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.160
But as we shall see,
it's an important concept

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:51.915
to keep in mind as
we're running a POC.

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:06.140
All right, so in
Spanner, the primary key

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:08.960
of each table in a
database is partitioned

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.640
into chunks called splits.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:14.000
So in this example, the
primary key is an integer.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:18.690
And the first split ranges from
minus infinity to minus 100,

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:25.420
and the next split from
minus 99 to 3,000, and so on.

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:27.900
So the same basic things
happens for other types,

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:32.010
such as strings and floats and
for multicolored primary keys.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:38.730
Now, each zone has a
copy of each split.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:41.460
So each zone will have
a copy of split 0.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:43.950
It appears in different
places on different servers

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.480
in each zone.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.340
But the key point
is that each zone

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:50.670
has a full copy of the
key space of the table

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:54.350
and indeed has a full
copy of the table's data.

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:57.610
You can think of each zone
as a standalone replica.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:00.850
And Spanner ensures that
each replica stays in sync

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:03.670
and has exactly the
same committed data.

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:06.250
So what's the role of splits?

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:09.670
First, splits are the basis
for distributing tables

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:11.890
across span servers.

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:16.030
And second, they form the core
part of an automated sharding

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:19.390
mechanism to balance
load across span servers.

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:21.040
So splits are not static.

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:22.700
They're continually on the move.

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:24.320
They're continually evolving.

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:27.790
So for example, if a split is
hot and receives lots of load

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:29.650
and contains lots
of data, it will

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:31.690
be split into smaller splits.

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:33.520
If two neighboring
splits are relatively

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:36.400
inactive for a long period of
time, and they're small enough,

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:38.920
they'll eventually be merged.

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:44.200
Now, it's worth emphasizing
that the starting point

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:48.200
for an empty table
is just one split.

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:52.360
So when load starts landing
on that table, all of it

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:54.250
is going to hit that one split.

00:24:54.250 --> 00:24:57.220
In other words, all
traffic for that one table

00:24:57.220 --> 00:25:00.790
will go to just three span
servers, one from each zone.

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:02.890
Now, suppose I have a
hundred nodes provisioned

00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:04.540
for my Spanner instance.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:07.900
Then my capacity when I
start with an empty table

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:11.410
is effectively 1 node
out of 100, or just 1%

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:12.940
of my total capacity.

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:16.540
Now, the good news is that
Spanner quickly adapts.

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:21.320
It can handle 2x increases
in load in a few minutes.

00:25:21.320 --> 00:25:23.110
But when starting
from an empty table,

00:25:23.110 --> 00:25:25.960
we need several rounds
of such increases

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:27.970
to get to full
capacity, and we'll

00:25:27.970 --> 00:25:33.240
see this process in some
detail later in the talk.

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:37.350
All right, so let's
switch back to POCs.

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:39.510
And I want to talk
a bit about why

00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:42.420
splits are important for POCs.

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:47.738
So they're really
important for performance.

00:25:47.738 --> 00:25:49.530
You know, as we've
noted, splits are really

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:50.830
important for performance.

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:54.360
And if I have two databases
with exactly the same data

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:56.970
but different sets
of splits, then I'll

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:59.070
probably get
different performance.

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:01.440
If one database just
has a couple of splits,

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:05.340
and the other has a lot more
splits, maybe 10x or 100x more,

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:08.490
then we'll probably see
substantial differences

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:10.870
in performance between
those two databases.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:14.910
So that means to get
repeatable results in a POC,

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:17.340
I need to set up similar splits.

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:20.610
Or to put it another way, I
need to have a warmup procedure

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:22.380
that I consistently use.

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:25.950
And without this, you risk
getting skewed results that

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:28.180
are confusing and misleading.

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:31.330
Now, as we'll see, doing a
warmup isn't that difficult.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:33.930
It's mostly about
doing it consistently

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:35.175
to get consistent results.

00:26:37.900 --> 00:26:40.120
All right, let's jump
to some experiments.

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:49.460
The first experiment is about
scalability and latency.

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:51.410
And we're going to start
with an empty table,

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:55.280
then load some data, and then
send some team update requests.

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:57.290
And we'll see the
effect of splits.

00:26:57.290 --> 00:27:01.580
So we'll see effectively the
process of doing a warmup.

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:04.520
The second experiment is
about handling overload

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:05.900
by scaling up.

00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:07.790
And the last experiment
is about handling

00:27:07.790 --> 00:27:10.040
more expensive
analytics queries that

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:13.250
involve doing substantial
scans over the database.

00:27:13.250 --> 00:27:15.500
We're going to look at the
performance of such queries

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:17.660
and how they relate to splits.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:20.120
And we're going to use a
query to actually find out

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:22.835
which quarterbacks are the most
popular in our fantasy teams.

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:29.230
Let's start with the
first experiment.

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:32.410
As I mentioned, we're going
to start with an empty table.

00:27:32.410 --> 00:27:34.190
We're going to load some data.

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:36.490
And then we're going to
send update team requests.

00:27:36.490 --> 00:27:38.215
So the initial
load happened here.

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:46.460
The initial load was doing
large bulk writes in Spanner.

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:48.210
And they didn't really
show up in the QPS.

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:50.460
So the QPS with
this was very small

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:54.390
because we were sending large
sets of writes to Spanner.

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:56.220
So the QPS was
probably under about 1

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:58.590
QPS, just doesn't
show up on this graph.

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:05.257
About 10 minutes
after the load--

00:28:05.257 --> 00:28:07.590
so we did the initial load,
waited for about 10 minutes,

00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:09.900
and then we started
sending update traffic.

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:12.600
And as you can see,
our QPS capacity

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:17.940
increases fairly quickly,
reaching about 100%

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:22.380
after about 45 minutes, although
we do see some QPS variation.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:28.310
Then it becomes very
stable after about an hour.

00:28:28.310 --> 00:28:29.870
And so about an hour
after the start,

00:28:29.870 --> 00:28:33.920
we're reliably
seeing about 55,000,

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:40.130
56,000 57,000 QPS of update
team traffic, all right?

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:42.380
Let's push this graph to
the bottom of our slide

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:47.330
to make room for a CPU graph.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:52.800
And when we say CPU,
we're particularly

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:57.690
interested in CPU utilization
of high-priority operations.

00:28:57.690 --> 00:29:03.870
So a strong recommendation is to
keep this below this 65% level.

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:11.510
Now, you can see that
CPU is growing over time

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:18.810
and that we stabilize around
42% to 43% CPU utilization.

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:22.110
So what's going on during
this ramp-up period?

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:24.420
Well, at the start
of this period,

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:28.710
all the load is hitting only a
small number of span servers,

00:29:28.710 --> 00:29:30.630
and they're likely to
be running very hot.

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:33.360
So those particular span
servers are probably

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:35.850
running at 100% utilization.

00:29:35.850 --> 00:29:38.260
And that's what's
limiting our QPS.

00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:40.797
Now, many of our span
servers at this point

00:29:40.797 --> 00:29:42.630
are going to be seeing
little or no traffic.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:44.850
They're basically
going to be idle.

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:48.280
Now, the overall utilization--

00:29:48.280 --> 00:29:50.400
so the overall
utilization at this point

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.610
over the entire instance is low.

00:29:53.610 --> 00:29:58.800
Over time, we even out the
load, and more spans servers

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:00.120
see more load.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.160
And eventually, this
load is balanced,

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:06.880
and we see an even spread of
the load across span servers.

00:30:06.880 --> 00:30:10.900
So that pretty much explains
the growth in CPU utilization

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:17.700
and how that tracks fairly
closely the growth in QPS.

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:22.650
Now, early on, I mentioned
the initial load of users,

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:24.730
and it didn't show
up on our QPS graph

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:26.710
since the QPS was so small.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:29.670
But we do see it
on the CPU graph,

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:33.180
and it's just a little
blip just before 2:30.

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:38.190
So one conclusion here is that
the initial load of user data

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:39.810
was just a tiny blip.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:44.300
It didn't take long to do, and
it didn't use many resources.

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:50.520
And now we mentioned that--

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:54.210
earlier, we mentioned
that Spanner splits

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:57.450
are done based on-- the
load balancing is done based

00:30:57.450 --> 00:31:01.290
on the size of data and
splits and the load, basically

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:03.390
CPU on each split.

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:05.640
And in this case,
our initial load

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:08.610
didn't have enough
data or enough CPU load

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:10.560
and wasn't running
for long enough

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:14.440
to really stimulate the split
and load balancing mechanisms.

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:18.150
So this initial load
didn't really load balance.

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:20.160
It didn't really
establish many splits.

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:22.360
It did a little bit, but
it didn't do too much.

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:26.695
And so the real warmup happened
when we added the update teams

00:31:26.695 --> 00:31:27.195
traffic.

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:36.520
So if our initial load had
included much more data,

00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:39.520
had taken longer,
and used more CPU,

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:42.040
then it's likely the
shape of our QPS graph

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:43.480
would look very different.

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:47.140
And we would have seen a much
faster QPS ramp-up, potentially

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:49.070
an immediate QPS ramp-up.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:52.210
So this is something to think
about for our actual migration.

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:54.490
How much data will we
be loading initially?

00:31:54.490 --> 00:31:57.070
And maybe we should
model that in our POC.

00:31:57.070 --> 00:32:01.180
We'll come back to this
point a little bit later.

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:04.290
All right, let's switch
over to look at latency.

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:11.220
We've dropped the CPU
graph from the top,

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:13.230
and we've replaced
it with latency.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:17.640
But we're keeping the underlying
QPS graph as a reference point.

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:21.930
So what we see is that
latency almost looks

00:32:21.930 --> 00:32:24.030
like a mirror image of QPS.

00:32:24.030 --> 00:32:25.920
Initially, latency
is high, and then

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:28.830
it significantly reduces
towards the end of warmup

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:33.720
as QPS stabilizes to
around about 56,000 QPS.

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:35.970
Let's dig a little
bit deeper into this.

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:40.110
The latency graph
actually has four lines.

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:44.340
It provides both 50th
percentile and 99th percentile

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:47.190
for both reads and writes.

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:49.470
And it's the 99th
percentile latency

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:50.880
that is huge at startup.

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:53.760
So note that the scale here
is actually in seconds.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:56.490
So some of the latencies
were 99th percentile here

00:32:56.490 --> 00:33:00.060
during startup are really huge.

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:03.910
Now, once we reach steady state,
and the warmup, is complete,

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:07.170
we see that latencies
are much lower, right?

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:11.730
They're about 10 milliseconds at
median, 19 at 99th percentile.

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:14.940
And reads are about 2
milliseconds at median

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:21.660
and around 6.6 milliseconds
at 99th percentile.

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:25.170
Now, it's a little hard to
see the behavior of latency

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:25.780
over here.

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:29.990
So I'm going to zoom
in and just look

00:33:29.990 --> 00:33:32.190
at the latency in that
chunk of the graph,

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:34.920
so basically after
warmup's complete.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:36.560
So we can see a
little more about what

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:39.920
are the variations here
after warmup's complete

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.300
and give a more complete
picture of latency.

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:44.960
So after warmup,
our median latencies

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:46.310
for reads and writes--

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:49.010
so these two, the purple
line here and the green line

00:33:49.010 --> 00:33:52.100
here-- are really solid
at about 2 milliseconds

00:33:52.100 --> 00:33:54.290
and 10 milliseconds
respectively.

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:57.170
99th percentile bounces
around a little bit

00:33:57.170 --> 00:34:01.360
but is mostly under 6
milliseconds for reads

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:05.560
and 20 milliseconds for writes.

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:08.230
So these are pretty solid
numbers and fairly typical.

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:12.460
And note that we're running here
at about 43% CPU utilization.

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:18.810
All right, in
experiment 2, we're

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:22.770
going to consider what happens
if we overload the system.

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:24.900
So we're going to
crank up utilization

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:26.433
using a warmed-up database.

00:34:26.433 --> 00:34:28.350
So we've already applied
our warmup procedure.

00:34:28.350 --> 00:34:30.690
We've already established
all of the splits.

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:34.560
Our database is ready to handle
substantial amounts of traffic.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:37.679
And we're going to crank
utilization up to over 65%.

00:34:37.679 --> 00:34:39.179
And we're basically
going to do this

00:34:39.179 --> 00:34:41.699
by reducing the
number of nodes to 12.

00:34:41.699 --> 00:34:43.650
So that's in this chunk
of the graph here.

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:48.300
We can see we're running around
about 65%, sometimes over 65%

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:49.900
here.

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:57.090
And then what we want to do is
understand how quickly Spanner

00:34:57.090 --> 00:34:59.033
adjusts when we add more nodes.

00:34:59.033 --> 00:35:00.450
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:04.080
going to increase the number
of nodes from 12 to 30.

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:06.570
And as expected,
the CPU utilization

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:11.100
drops substantially and
pretty much immediately.

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:13.770
But we're probably more
interested in the question

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:16.880
of what actually
happens to latency.

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:20.350
So to pack all of
this on one slide,

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:23.020
we'll drop the number
of nodes, and we'll

00:35:23.020 --> 00:35:25.840
use CPU utilization
as our reference graph

00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:28.990
and move it to the
top of the slide.

00:35:28.990 --> 00:35:33.190
Now, let's add
50th-percentile latency.

00:35:33.190 --> 00:35:35.020
And you can see
that 50th percentile

00:35:35.020 --> 00:35:37.570
for both reads and
writes doesn't actually

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:38.710
change very much.

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:40.240
There's a little
bit of ripple here

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:44.230
to the left when we're running
at very high utilization.

00:35:44.230 --> 00:35:47.500
But mostly, we're solid at
around about 2 milliseconds

00:35:47.500 --> 00:35:51.700
for reads and 10 to 11
milliseconds for writes.

00:35:51.700 --> 00:35:54.100
It's a much more
interesting story when

00:35:54.100 --> 00:35:55.840
we look at 99th percentile.

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:58.060
And here, the
change is dramatic.

00:35:58.060 --> 00:36:02.670
When we were running
at high utilization,

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:05.820
99th percentile
latencies were up

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:07.690
in the hundreds of milliseconds.

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:13.460
But after the increase in nodes,
they drop back by 10x to 30x.

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:18.280
So a couple of
things to note here.

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:21.710
First on the right-hand side,
we see that write latency 99th

00:36:21.710 --> 00:36:25.200
percentile over on this
side of the graph--

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:29.670
write latency 99th percentile
is about 2x read latency.

00:36:29.670 --> 00:36:32.640
But on the other side over
here, the two latencies

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:34.350
are actually very similar.

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:36.090
And the likely
explanation for this

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:37.620
is that the
latencies on the left

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:39.330
are dominated by
queuings effects,

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:42.790
not on the actual
operation executed.

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:46.140
The second note that the
CPU utilization dropped

00:36:46.140 --> 00:36:49.380
when we increased the
nodes, and it dropped just

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:52.770
before about 8:15.

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:57.300
But the 99th percentile
didn't drop till a little bit

00:36:57.300 --> 00:36:58.590
after 8:15.

00:36:58.590 --> 00:37:01.830
So this 7 to 8-minute
difference is basically

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:05.040
the time for Spanner
to adjust and balance

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:08.730
the splits from hot span
servers to the new span servers

00:37:08.730 --> 00:37:11.040
that we've added when we
increase the node count.

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:19.150
All right, the last experiment
is sample analytic query.

00:37:19.150 --> 00:37:22.750
And basically, we want
to see which quarterback

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:24.770
is picked more frequently.

00:37:24.770 --> 00:37:27.820
And this is essentially a full
table scan and an aggregation.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:34.120
What we're going to do is
look at the performance

00:37:34.120 --> 00:37:37.150
of this query right after
the initial data load

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:39.370
and then again after warmup.

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:40.900
And a critical
tool to understand

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:42.760
the performance of
these kinds of queries

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:45.797
is query explanations.

00:37:45.797 --> 00:37:47.755
And we'll dig into this
in the next few slides.

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:59.240
OK, so when you run a query
using Spanner's Pantheon UI,

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:03.140
you get a page like this,
and the query is here.

00:38:03.140 --> 00:38:05.750
Now, I've added an
additional hint for Spanner

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:09.260
to use extra parallelism, this
"USE_ADDITIONAL_PARALLELISM"

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:10.920
here.

00:38:10.920 --> 00:38:13.160
And I'll come back
to this shortly.

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:14.370
For the moment, ignore it.

00:38:14.370 --> 00:38:17.900
It actually doesn't have too
much impact on the first query

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:19.670
that we're going to run.

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:22.190
Now, what I want
to highlight here

00:38:22.190 --> 00:38:24.440
is how to get to
query explanations.

00:38:24.440 --> 00:38:26.960
So when you run a
query, you usually

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:29.960
land on the results
for that query.

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:31.700
But to see the
query explanation,

00:38:31.700 --> 00:38:34.190
you need to click on
Explanation, which

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:37.220
I've circled in bright green.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:43.220
When you click on that, you
will see an Explanation panel.

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:47.850
And that Explanation
panel has a lot of data,

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:51.240
so we'll need to zoom in to
see it in a bit more detail.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:53.580
So here we are, zoomed
in to that panel.

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:55.910
Now, if we focus
on this panel, we

00:38:55.910 --> 00:38:58.230
see that it's made up
of three subpanels.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:02.640
First on the left, there is
a zoomed-in view of the plan.

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:06.620
So we can see the
"Serialize result,"

00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:09.350
which is the top box
of the query plan.

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:13.370
The next is a zoomed-out view
that includes the entire plan,

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:15.440
although it's kind
of too small for us

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:17.090
to really read carefully.

00:39:17.090 --> 00:39:20.030
And we can see this square
here that shows what's

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:22.910
included in the zoomed-in view.

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:26.190
OK, and on the right
is the third panel,

00:39:26.190 --> 00:39:29.060
which includes lots
of stats on the query.

00:39:29.060 --> 00:39:32.150
Now, in this shade
of green, I've

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:35.210
highlighted some
interesting stats.

00:39:35.210 --> 00:39:39.890
Our query took around about
321 milliseconds to run.

00:39:39.890 --> 00:39:41.870
This is 322 milliseconds to run.

00:39:41.870 --> 00:39:46.850
And it consumed about 581, just
over about 582 milliseconds

00:39:46.850 --> 00:39:47.570
of CPU.

00:39:47.570 --> 00:39:49.070
So how can that be?

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:51.470
We'll dig a little bit
further into the plan.

00:39:51.470 --> 00:39:54.500
And we're going to do that
by basically dragging down

00:39:54.500 --> 00:40:00.530
this box and then clicking on
this bottom-most element, which

00:40:00.530 --> 00:40:01.800
is the table scan.

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:03.720
So we've done that, and
this is what we see.

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:04.970
So we've dragged down the box.

00:40:04.970 --> 00:40:06.680
We've clicked on Table Scans.

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:10.960
And what we see,
interestingly, here

00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:16.150
is that the right-hand
panel gives us

00:40:16.150 --> 00:40:17.980
the stats for the table scan.

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:20.740
And note that there
are two scan instances

00:40:20.740 --> 00:40:22.100
in this particular case.

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:24.710
So these ran in parallel.

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:28.540
And that's why the query ran
for about 322 milliseconds

00:40:28.540 --> 00:40:32.260
but consumed 582
milliseconds of CPU.

00:40:32.260 --> 00:40:35.620
It also indicates that we've
just got a couple of splits

00:40:35.620 --> 00:40:36.380
in the table.

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:42.370
So this first run of the query
we did just after warmup.

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:44.230
Sorry, let me
rephrase that-- just

00:40:44.230 --> 00:40:46.600
after we'd loaded the
data, so before we did

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:48.740
the main part of the warmup.

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:51.325
Now let's run the query
again after warmup.

00:40:54.340 --> 00:40:57.790
First, note that the
query only took just

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:01.330
over 100 milliseconds,
and it was 322 before.

00:41:01.330 --> 00:41:04.570
Now we want to dig into
the table scan operation

00:41:04.570 --> 00:41:08.067
at the bottom of the plan.

00:41:08.067 --> 00:41:09.150
And here's what we'll see.

00:41:09.150 --> 00:41:12.820
So we now have 77 executions.

00:41:12.820 --> 00:41:14.850
So there's a bunch we
could discuss here.

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:17.650
I just want to hit
a few key points.

00:41:17.650 --> 00:41:22.620
So the key message is that there
are more instances of the table

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:25.360
scan operation, which means
we've got more parallelism,

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:27.040
so the query is faster.

00:41:27.040 --> 00:41:29.220
So very roughly,
the more splits you

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:32.140
have, the more
table scan instances

00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:34.390
you'll see for this query.

00:41:34.390 --> 00:41:37.500
So if the latency for this
kind of query is important,

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:39.360
then ensure that
your database is

00:41:39.360 --> 00:41:42.240
warmed up before you start
doing performance analysis

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:44.170
on these kinds of queries.

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:47.130
Now, I promised
before to explain

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:54.550
a bit about what the
"USE_ADDITIONAL_PARALLELISM"

00:41:54.550 --> 00:41:56.500
hint was about.

00:41:56.500 --> 00:42:00.460
So this hint tells Spanner to
maximize available parallelism

00:42:00.460 --> 00:42:02.050
when there are lots of splits.

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:05.380
And without it, Spanner will
limit the amount of parallelism

00:42:05.380 --> 00:42:06.230
it uses.

00:42:06.230 --> 00:42:08.260
And this is really a
protection mechanism

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:11.710
to ensure that one large
query with lots of parallelism

00:42:11.710 --> 00:42:15.080
doesn't monopolize the
resources of a Spanner instance.

00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:18.310
But in this case, our
query is not that large,

00:42:18.310 --> 00:42:20.230
and we're interested
in making it fast,

00:42:20.230 --> 00:42:22.090
so it's appropriate
to use the flag here.

00:42:22.090 --> 00:42:26.050
But obviously, you need to
use this flag with care.

00:42:28.850 --> 00:42:30.890
All right, so that's
all of the experiments

00:42:30.890 --> 00:42:34.040
that I wanted to discuss.

00:42:34.040 --> 00:42:38.360
But before I conclude, I want
to go back to the POC plan

00:42:38.360 --> 00:42:40.790
that we mapped out at the
start of our discussion

00:42:40.790 --> 00:42:43.170
and basically to do
a critique of it.

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:44.130
So here's the plan--

00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:45.830
so this is an exact
copy of the slide

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:49.080
that we had earlier in the talk.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:51.330
Now, some of the
limitations that we have

00:42:51.330 --> 00:42:52.890
were kind of clear up front.

00:42:52.890 --> 00:42:56.220
But some of the
experiments also helped

00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:58.690
us to understand the
limitations of this plan

00:42:58.690 --> 00:43:01.230
and some potential things
we might like to tweak

00:43:01.230 --> 00:43:02.590
if we were to do it again.

00:43:02.590 --> 00:43:06.720
So first, we saw that
the million active users

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:09.720
was kind of on the small side.

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:12.270
And it led us to a
quite small initial data

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:15.960
set with only a few
splits after the load.

00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:20.880
So part of the issue here was
that in designing the POC,

00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:23.880
we ramped up the QPS--

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:27.270
so we went from 5,000 or
10,000 to saying, OK now,

00:43:27.270 --> 00:43:29.970
POC, we want to do 50,000.

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:32.820
We should probably have ramped
up the number of users as well.

00:43:32.820 --> 00:43:35.550
Maybe 10 million users
or 50 million users

00:43:35.550 --> 00:43:36.750
might have been reasonable.

00:43:39.760 --> 00:43:45.880
OK, so next, let's consider
the scope of the POC.

00:43:45.880 --> 00:43:49.210
So we ran just one
week of the season.

00:43:49.210 --> 00:43:53.530
But the question is, will
this work in week 18?

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:56.410
So we'll have a lot
more data in the table.

00:43:56.410 --> 00:43:58.750
Point-wise lookups
should be fine,

00:43:58.750 --> 00:44:03.900
but scans will be
more expensive.

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:08.450
Finally, let's consider
how we structure our runs.

00:44:08.450 --> 00:44:12.710
So basically, we generated load
and then ran our team updates

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:13.730
for week 1.

00:44:13.730 --> 00:44:17.040
But what about the rollover
from one week to the next?

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:19.640
So potentially, it's
worth tweaking our runs

00:44:19.640 --> 00:44:22.940
so that we generate
data over a full season

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:27.290
and actually do the rollover
of how the team for one week

00:44:27.290 --> 00:44:29.090
is essentially
inherited from the team

00:44:29.090 --> 00:44:30.200
from the previous week.

00:44:33.850 --> 00:44:36.810
So the overall point
here is that in a POC,

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:38.970
we typically make
simplifications

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:42.900
of the production system and
just focus on core systems

00:44:42.900 --> 00:44:44.190
and functionality.

00:44:44.190 --> 00:44:45.560
And this is a tradeoff.

00:44:45.560 --> 00:44:50.110
Fewer simplifications mean
that there's more work to do.

00:44:50.110 --> 00:44:51.390
There's a longer POC.

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:53.100
And potentially,
we get overwhelmed

00:44:53.100 --> 00:44:56.670
with complexity, kind of missing
the forest for the trees.

00:44:56.670 --> 00:44:59.160
On the other hand,
more simplifications

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:02.290
mean we might skip or
miss important issues.

00:45:02.290 --> 00:45:05.610
So it's kind of useful to
list out the simplifications

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:07.110
you make and critique them.

00:45:07.110 --> 00:45:09.270
So I've listed a number
of the simplifications

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:12.730
we made in the POC here,
roughly in order of importance.

00:45:12.730 --> 00:45:14.413
We've already talked
about some of them.

00:45:14.413 --> 00:45:16.080
But one of them I
wanted to mention here

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:18.720
is that all of the latency
numbers we were measuring

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:20.070
were measured at Spanner.

00:45:20.070 --> 00:45:23.400
And probably, we
should be measuring

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:26.100
some of those latencies
both at the application

00:45:26.100 --> 00:45:28.320
as well as potentially
at the user side.

00:45:32.100 --> 00:45:36.240
All right, I want
to briefly recap

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:39.520
some of the best
practices we've discussed.

00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:44.010
So the first is to collect
reliable and repeatable

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:46.350
results, make sure
that your database

00:45:46.350 --> 00:45:48.060
is appropriately warmed up.

00:45:48.060 --> 00:45:50.370
It's good to have a
standard warmup procedure

00:45:50.370 --> 00:45:53.580
and to use this regularly
as part of your experiment

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:55.530
protocol.

00:45:55.530 --> 00:46:01.230
Secondly, don't run Spanner or
your apps too hot, especially

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:03.010
if you're taking
latency measurements.

00:46:03.010 --> 00:46:06.240
So in general, we recommend
sort of 40% to 50%

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:10.290
is a good target for
Spanner utilization.

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:13.810
And third, get familiar
with query explanations.

00:46:13.810 --> 00:46:15.360
These are a really
effective tool

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:20.030
for debugging and understanding
query performance.

00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:24.350
OK, I want to make one
final comment on tooling.

00:46:24.350 --> 00:46:28.460
And it's fairly common
in a POC to use data

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:32.390
from an existing database
and bring that into your POC.

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:34.430
And we've built a
tool to help you

00:46:34.430 --> 00:46:37.100
move such data sets
from MySQL or Postgres

00:46:37.100 --> 00:46:38.750
to Spanner automatically.

00:46:38.750 --> 00:46:40.310
It's called HarbourBridge.

00:46:40.310 --> 00:46:43.550
And in our POC, we could
have used this to move over

00:46:43.550 --> 00:46:46.340
a set of users and their
teams from an existing

00:46:46.340 --> 00:46:50.390
database to Spanner instead
of randomly generating them.

00:46:50.390 --> 00:46:53.390
So HarbourBridge provides
the following functionality--

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:56.450
it ingests data from
MySQL or Postgres,

00:46:56.450 --> 00:47:00.650
either using dump or directly
connecting to your database.

00:47:00.650 --> 00:47:02.743
It builds a Spanner
schema automatically.

00:47:02.743 --> 00:47:04.160
And this is something
that you can

00:47:04.160 --> 00:47:06.960
browse and customize
using the Schema Assistant

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:08.180
mode of HarbourBridge.

00:47:08.180 --> 00:47:10.910
It creates a new Spanner
database with the schema

00:47:10.910 --> 00:47:13.790
and populates it with data
from the source database.

00:47:13.790 --> 00:47:17.100
And it also documents what it
did with a detailed assessment

00:47:17.100 --> 00:47:17.600
report.

00:47:17.600 --> 00:47:20.270
So it identifies
issues, tells you

00:47:20.270 --> 00:47:22.400
about features that
weren't mapped to Spanner,

00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:25.280
and tells you about any
errors that are encountered.

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:31.290
All right, let me stop here
and take any questions.

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:31.955
Let me check.

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:38.930
Let me check on the--

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:40.730
let's see-- on the chat.

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:43.150
Where are we?

00:47:43.150 --> 00:47:49.970
OK, so Thiago asked, "Why
is this only reflected

00:47:49.970 --> 00:47:52.720
in the 99th percentile?"

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:54.710
Thiago, do you mind
clarifying that question?

00:47:54.710 --> 00:47:55.070
Do you mean--

00:47:55.070 --> 00:47:55.390
THIAGO TASCA NUNES: Yeah, sure.

00:47:55.390 --> 00:47:56.520
NEVIN HEINTZE: --why is--

00:47:56.520 --> 00:47:57.730
yeah, go ahead.

00:47:57.730 --> 00:48:00.310
THIAGO TASCA NUNES: So yeah,
I was asking about the--

00:48:00.310 --> 00:48:03.370
when you reduced
the number of nodes,

00:48:03.370 --> 00:48:06.710
you kind of saw the effect
only on the 99th percentile,

00:48:06.710 --> 00:48:09.110
but not on the other percentile.

00:48:09.110 --> 00:48:11.380
So I was wondering why
is that only reflected

00:48:11.380 --> 00:48:13.930
on the [? tail ?] [? ends. ?]

00:48:13.930 --> 00:48:15.940
NEVIN HEINTZE: So
basically, when

00:48:15.940 --> 00:48:19.870
you're running a system
very close to its limits,

00:48:19.870 --> 00:48:23.085
many operations are still
going to perform OK.

00:48:23.085 --> 00:48:24.460
But occasionally,
there are going

00:48:24.460 --> 00:48:27.820
to be some operations that
are going to get caught up

00:48:27.820 --> 00:48:31.960
and basically get descheduled
or don't get scheduled

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:36.817
at an appropriate time because
the system is basically really,

00:48:36.817 --> 00:48:37.900
really close to the limit.

00:48:37.900 --> 00:48:41.290
So it's not uncommon for
your typical operation

00:48:41.290 --> 00:48:44.590
to still run fairly quickly.

00:48:44.590 --> 00:48:46.510
But it kind of depends
on the queuing model,

00:48:46.510 --> 00:48:48.135
and it depends on
lots of other things.

00:48:48.135 --> 00:48:50.710
So for instance,
if all queries had

00:48:50.710 --> 00:48:54.400
to go through a FIFO queue
that was centralized,

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:59.440
then we kind of expect that that
queuing latency would show up

00:48:59.440 --> 00:49:01.180
for basically all queries.

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:05.160
But that's not the way
that Spanner works.

00:49:05.160 --> 00:49:07.030
You know, Spanner--
you basically

00:49:07.030 --> 00:49:11.620
are sending queries across a
whole lot of different span

00:49:11.620 --> 00:49:12.340
servers.

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:14.710
And there's going to
be slight variations

00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:18.790
in the loading of those
different span servers.

00:49:18.790 --> 00:49:21.340
And so many of them
will be perfectly fine.

00:49:21.340 --> 00:49:26.590
They'll be at maybe 50%,
55% utilization, maybe 60%.

00:49:26.590 --> 00:49:28.690
And they'll be able to
handle your load just fine.

00:49:28.690 --> 00:49:34.840
Some span servers might be
up at 70%, 80% utilization,

00:49:34.840 --> 00:49:41.450
and maybe many of the operations
on those will be slower.

00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:45.580
So if we push the
utilization up to 80% or 90%,

00:49:45.580 --> 00:49:50.860
then I'd expect to see that
the latency at 50th percentile

00:49:50.860 --> 00:49:54.550
might start getting affected,
or at least the latency effects

00:49:54.550 --> 00:49:56.530
of the overload would
start pushing down

00:49:56.530 --> 00:49:58.180
to lower percentiles.

00:49:58.180 --> 00:49:59.557
THIAGO TASCA NUNES: OK.

00:49:59.557 --> 00:50:00.890
Yeah, that answered my question.

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:03.260
Thank you, Nevin.

00:50:03.260 --> 00:50:05.690
NEVIN HEINTZE: All right,
any other questions?

00:50:12.935 --> 00:50:15.060
THIAGO TASCA NUNES: Maybe
I can ask something else.

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:16.860
NEVIN HEINTZE: Absolutely.

00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:19.920
THIAGO TASCA NUNES: Did
you ever face a situation

00:50:19.920 --> 00:50:22.440
where a customer
would like to model

00:50:22.440 --> 00:50:25.080
a larger part of
the application,

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:28.170
not only kind of
this small scope?

00:50:28.170 --> 00:50:31.330
And how do you deal with that?

00:50:31.330 --> 00:50:34.350
NEVIN HEINTZE: So I
wouldn't say we've

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:37.030
modeled just a small
part of the application.

00:50:37.030 --> 00:50:41.670
This is really a critical
part of the application.

00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:43.860
And I think it's
quite common to pick

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:46.080
one of the most
important workloads

00:50:46.080 --> 00:50:53.010
in your POC, so something that
is highest QPS, most latency

00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:57.180
sensitive, most
important for users,

00:50:57.180 --> 00:51:01.230
for example, most important
part of user experience.

00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:03.310
So I wouldn't say we
pick a small part.

00:51:03.310 --> 00:51:05.040
What we're trying to
do is pick probably

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:07.338
the most important part.

00:51:07.338 --> 00:51:08.880
THIAGO TASCA NUNES:
Yeah, wrong word.

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:09.210
NEVIN HEINTZE: Yeah.

00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:10.680
Yeah, yeah, I just
want to emphasize

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:13.180
we're not trying to make this
problem really, really simple.

00:51:13.180 --> 00:51:16.020
It's we're trying to pick
perhaps the hardest problem

00:51:16.020 --> 00:51:19.656
and then go dig into that.

00:51:19.656 --> 00:51:23.070
You know, I think the challenge
of picking too many problems

00:51:23.070 --> 00:51:28.080
is that the POC can stretch on
for an extended period of time.

00:51:30.442 --> 00:51:31.650
But maybe that's appropriate.

00:51:31.650 --> 00:51:34.020
Maybe there are
five or six issues

00:51:34.020 --> 00:51:35.850
that you really need
to drill down into.

00:51:35.850 --> 00:51:38.790
So at some level,
a POC can almost

00:51:38.790 --> 00:51:41.268
morph into the actual migration.

00:51:41.268 --> 00:51:43.560
And sometimes, you don't
really know where the POC ends

00:51:43.560 --> 00:51:45.810
and the actual migration starts.

00:51:45.810 --> 00:51:50.610
But my feeling is that you're
much better off having a fairly

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:54.060
well-defined POC,
picking what you think

00:51:54.060 --> 00:51:56.700
are the really critical
parts of the system to model

00:51:56.700 --> 00:51:58.860
and just focusing on that.

00:51:58.860 --> 00:52:00.060
You know, there's nothing--

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:01.920
we'll just say this--

00:52:01.920 --> 00:52:05.610
if a customer particularly
wants to model many parts

00:52:05.610 --> 00:52:08.010
of the system, then sure.

00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:12.000
I mean, that's something that we
would always support them with.

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:18.475
But it potentially can
consume a lot of resources

00:52:18.475 --> 00:52:20.100
and take a considerable
amount of time.

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:22.650
So it's all about
focusing on what's

00:52:22.650 --> 00:52:25.350
the best value for the
time and investment you

00:52:25.350 --> 00:52:28.215
want to put into the POC.

00:52:28.215 --> 00:52:29.340
THIAGO TASCA NUNES: Got it.

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:29.910
Thank you.

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:39.750
NEVIN HEINTZE: All right,
any other questions?

00:53:15.015 --> 00:53:16.640
ROY FULLER: All right,
looks like there

00:53:16.640 --> 00:53:19.080
are no other questions.

00:53:19.080 --> 00:53:21.380
So I want to thank
you very much, Nevin,

00:53:21.380 --> 00:53:23.180
for hosting this session.

00:53:23.180 --> 00:53:25.890
And thank you,
everyone, for joining.

00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:27.890
I will end the
recording right now.

00:53:27.890 --> 00:53:28.550
Thank you all.

00:53:28.550 --> 00:53:31.603
Have a great morning,
afternoon, and evening.

00:53:31.603 --> 00:53:33.270
NEVIN HEINTZE: Thank
you very much, Roy.

00:53:33.270 --> 00:53:35.090
ROY FULLER: Thank you.

